; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Impossible Triangle
; CATEGORY: Shapes & Polygons
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     optical illusion, penrose, isometric grid, rotation, filled
; SUMMARY:  Draws a Penrose-style impossible triangle from six shaded strips on a triangular grid.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Three beams seem to meet at right angles to make a triangle,
;   but each beam passes in front of the next one all the way round,
;   which no real object could do. The picture is only six flat
;   strips in three colors. Your brain does the rest.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Change SIZE (how many grid steps along each side),
;   THICK (the width of each face) or the three SHADES.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * a triangular grid: points written as (a, b) steps along two
;     directions 60 degrees apart
;   * turning a whole shape by 120 degrees with a simple rule
;     on its grid coordinates
;   * how shading makes flat shapes look solid
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Make THICK 1.5 or 0.6. When does the illusion stop working?
;   * Give all six strips the same color. Is it still impossible?
;   * Swap two of the SHADES and see which way the beams seem to turn.
; ============================================================

make "size 9
make "thick 1
make "unit 50
make "shades [[245 180 70] [210 90 60] [110 50 70]]

; Grid point (a, b) means: a steps to the right, then b steps along
; the direction 60 degrees up from that. These two procedures give
; its place on the screen, with the whole figure centered.
to gx :p
  output :unit * ((first :p) + (last :p) / 2 - :size / 2)
end
to gy :p
  output :unit * (last :p) * (sqrt 3) / 2 - :unit * :size * (sqrt 3) / 4
end

; Turning by 120 degrees round the middle of the big triangle moves
; corner (0, 0) to (size, 0), (size, 0) to (0, size), and so on.
; On the grid that turn is just this little formula.
to turn120 :p
  output list :size - (first :p) - (last :p) first :p
end

; Paint a polygon given as a list of grid points.
to strip :points :color
  penup setxy gx first :points gy first :points pendown
  filled :color [
    foreach butfirst :points [setxy gx ? gy ?]
    setxy gx first :points gy first :points
  ]
end

; One beam is the bottom of the triangle. It has an outer face
; (the strip next to the edge) and an inner face (the next strip in).
; Its right end is cut on a slant where the next beam covers it.
to beam :turns :outercolor :innercolor
  localmake "n :size
  localmake "w :thick
  localmake "outer (list [0 0] (list :n - 2 * :w 0) (list :n - 3 * :w :w) (list 0 :w))
  localmake "inner (list (list 0 :w) (list :n - 3 * :w :w) (list :n - 4 * :w 2 * :w) (list 0 2 * :w))
  ; spin both faces round to the side we want
  repeat :turns [
    make "outer map "turn120 :outer
    make "inner map "turn120 :inner
  ]
  strip :outer :outercolor
  strip :inner :innercolor
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [235 240 245]
setpencolor [40 30 40]
setpensize 2
; each beam's inner face has the same shade as the next beam's
; outer face: that shared shade is what tricks the eye at the corners
beam 0 item 1 :shades item 2 :shades
beam 1 item 2 :shades item 3 :shades
beam 2 item 3 :shades item 1 :shades
